## The following text has been annotated for parts of speech by students at NTU Singapore as part of a class project in AY 2025-26.
## Verses are extracted from the taggedPBC. Annotations have been developed with a combination of manual and automated approaches, with reference to existing literature and (where possible) native speakers.
## ISO 639-3: mbf, Language: Baba Malay, Number of verses: 21
## References: Ansaldo, U., Lim, L., & Mufwene, S. S. (2007). The sociolinguistic history of the Peranakans: What it tells us about “creolization.” In U. Ansaldo, S. Matthews, & L. Lim (Eds.), Deconstructing creole (pp. 203–226). John Benjamins. https://doi.org/10.1075/tsl.73.11ans ; Bakker, P. (2025). Review of A grammar of modern Baba Malay, by N. H. Lee. Linguistic Typology, 29(2), 433–445. https://doi.org/10.1515/lingty-2024-0074 ; Gwee, W. T. H. (2006). Baba Malay Dictionary: The first comprehensive compendium of Straits Chinese terms and expressions. Tuttle Publishing. https://nlb.overdrive.com/media/2259786 ; Lee, N. H. (2014). A grammar of Baba Malay with sociophonetic considerations (Doctoral dissertation, University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa). University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa ScholarSpace. https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/items/9ffe1df0-f0aa-41cb-9d58-ba35354bfeca ; Lee, N. H. (2018). Baba Malay: Diverging trends in two ecologies. Journal of Pidgin and Creole Languages, 33(1), 135–172. https://doi.org/10.1075/jpcl.00004.lee
